{
  "id": "affinity_publisher",
  "name": "Affinity Publisher 2",
  "kind": "app",
  "category": "Creative",
  "match": {
    "windows_exe": [
      "Publisher.exe"
    ],
    "macos_bundle": [
      "com.seriflabs.affinitypublisher2"
    ],
    "window_title": []
  },
  "sources": [
    "https://affinity.help/publisher2/en-US.lproj/pages/Workspace/shortcuts.html",
    "https://affinity.help/publisher2/en-US.lproj/pages/Workspace/customizingShortcuts.html"
  ],
  "note": "Every row of Serif's \"Keyboard shortcuts\" page in the Affinity Publisher 2 help. The context is the page's category with the trailing word \"shortcuts\" removed (Operations, File, Tools, Edit, Object control, Page control, Text, View, Selection, Workspace, Blend mode, Misc, macOS), plus the table's own qualifier where it has one (Curve drawing, Curve editing with Node Tool, Transforming, Navigation, Formatting, Typography, Special characters, Deleting). The page holds a Windows and a macOS variant of every row in the same HTML and picks one at load time; the web build's stylesheet only ever draws the macOS glyphs, so the Windows chords here are read from the Windows variant's own markup, in which Serif's Command tag is the Ctrl key. The page's \"macOS shortcuts\" table is emitted as macOS only; rows Serif gives only in the Windows variant (Home/End text navigation, Delete character to right of insertion point, Delete from insertion point to end of word) keep a Windows chord alone. Rows that document a set of keys - the arrow keys for nudging, [ and ] for stroke width - become one action per key with the chord repeated in parentheses. Left out because the chord grammar cannot express them: everything using a drag, a click, a right-click or the mouse scroll wheel (view rotation, vertical and horizontal scrolling, the four Hot key zoom rows, Isolate an object), and the opacity row Serif writes as a run of numeric keys (\"4=40%, 43=43%\"), along with the Fn-key row (Emoji & Symbols). \"Edit line strokes as you draw\" is left out because Serif's markup prints a bare modifier with no key, and \"Justify Left\" because Serif writes its key as the pipe character, which the grammar has no spelling for. Where Serif writes the key as \"+\" (the macOS form of Zoom In) it is kept as Plus rather than guessed at.",
